#!/usr/bin/env bash
# 60-moai-ports — give each human their own Mo AI front door, on one machine.
#
# THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES
#
# Mo AI is three small always-on USER services, each on a fixed loopback port:
#
#     moai-gateway    8080   the front door: routes to the brain, holds the key
#     moai-control    8079   machine state + this user's brain config
#     moai-agent-api  8077   this user's sessions, agent settings, Telegram token
#
# One human per machine, that is correct and simple. MoOS Cloud broke that
# assumption the day `moos-cloud-dev` started adding a second developer to a
# shared server, and nothing was updated to match. Measured on the maintainer's
# own VPS with two accounts logged in:
#
#   * moalfarras's moai-agent-api  — FAILED, "[Errno 98] Address already in use"
#   * momo's moai-control/gateway  — reported ACTIVE while spinning for ever on
#                                    "port 8080 busy, retrying in 2s". Green and
#                                    completely non-functional, which is the
#                                    failure mode this repo keeps re-learning.
#
# And the part that is not just a bug:
#
#   $ runuser -u momo -- curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/models
#   {"object": "list", "data": [...]}          ← answered
#
# 8080 was moalfarras's gateway. The second developer's Mo AI was talking to the
# FIRST developer's front door — and the gateway is, by its own design comment,
# the one component that "alone ever sees" the cloud API key. There is no local
# authentication to prevent it: X-Moai-Agent and X-Moai-Control are guards
# against drive-by web pages, not against another account on the same machine.
#
# WHY A GENERATOR AND NOT A PATCH TO THE SERVICES
#
# All three already read their port from the environment (MOAI_GATEWAY_PORT,
# MOAI_CONTROL_PORT, MOAI_AGENT_PORT). The knob existed; nothing ever turned it.
# A user-environment-generator runs once per user manager and injects into that
# user's whole session, so every service, the `moai` launcher and the app all
# agree without a line of Python changing.
#
# THE OFFSET, AND WHY UID 1000 IS DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED
#
#     port = base + (uid - 1000) * 100
#
# The first account on any MoOS install is uid 1000, and 1000 - 1000 = 0. So the
# maintainer's daily-driver desktop — one user, uid 1000 — keeps 8077/8079/8080
# exactly as before. This change is invisible on every single-user machine, which
# is the only responsible way to touch something the whole of Mo AI talks through.
# The second developer (uid 1001) gets 8177/8179/8180.
#
# A stride of 100 rather than 1: the bases are 3 apart and the local brain sits on
# 8081, so +1 would have walked user 1001's control port straight onto user 1000's
# gateway, and their gateway onto the brain.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* OFFSET: the local brain (MOAI_LOCAL_PORT / 8081).
# That one is SHARED on purpose — it is the only expensive piece, a whole model
# resident in RAM, and the owner's requirement is one brain for both people, not
# one each. Both users' gateways route local traffic to that single engine.

# A generator must print KEY=value lines and nothing else. Any noise here lands
# in the user manager's environment, so stay quiet on every failure path.
uid="$(id -u 2>/dev/null)" || exit 0
case "$uid" in ''|*[!0-9]*) exit 0 ;; esac

# System and dynamic users have no Mo AI session; leave their environment alone.
[ "$uid" -ge 1000 ] || exit 0

offset=$(( (uid - 1000) * 100 ))

# Past the tidy registered band the linear offset would walk into other services,
# but the ORIGINAL guard here did the one thing this file exists to prevent: for
# uid >= 1010 (offset > 900) it `exit 0`ed WITHOUT printing, so the three services
# fell back to their built-in defaults 8080/8079/8077 — exactly uid 1000's ports.
# The eleventh account thus reached the first account's gateway (and its cloud
# key), the very cross-tenant collision documented above. Exiting-without-printing
# is fail-OPEN.
#
# Fail CLOSED instead: fold the high end into a private, uid-keyed band that stays
# in valid port space and can NEVER equal uid 1000's base. A collision in this band
# (two accounts one full cycle apart sharing a slot) is at worst a user's OWN service
# failing to bind — visible and safe — never another tenant's front door.
#
# THE BAND IS DERIVED, NOT PICKED, and it must clear TWO ceilings:
#
#   1. A port is at most 65535.
#   2. A LISTENING port must stay out of the kernel's ephemeral range
#      (net.ipv4.ip_local_port_range, 32768-60999 on this machine). A service that
#      binds inside it races the kernel's own outgoing connections and fails
#      intermittently with EADDRINUSE — the worst kind of bug, because it depends on
#      what the machine happened to be doing a second earlier.
#
# So every port stays BELOW 32768. The linear band already does (8077..8980); the fold
# starts at 10000, clear of it, and the modulus follows from the ceiling:
#
#     8080 + 10000 + (N-1)*100 < 32768   ->   N <= 147
#
#     max port = 8080 + 10000 + 146*100 = 32680   (below the ephemeral floor)
#     min port = 8077 + 10000           = 18077   (never a base port)
#
# Two earlier attempts were wrong and are recorded so the next one is not:
#   * `% 400` from a 20000 base produced 8080+20000+39900 = 67980 for uid 1399 — not a
#     port at all, so that account's Mo AI could not bind.
#   * `% 350` fixed the ceiling but left 283 of its 350 slots inside the ephemeral
#     range, trading a hard failure for an intermittent one.
# 147 slots is far more than a single machine will ever have accounts, and the gate
# below proves EVERY uid rather than sampling — sampling is what let the first one ship.
if [ "$offset" -gt 900 ]; then
    offset=$(( 10000 + ((uid - 1000) % 147) * 100 ))
fi

printf 'MOAI_GATEWAY_PORT=%d\n' $(( 8080 + offset ))
printf 'MOAI_CONTROL_PORT=%d\n' $(( 8079 + offset ))
printf 'MOAI_AGENT_PORT=%d\n'   $(( 8077 + offset ))
